ike banks  have been on this for decades and  decades and decades right like they want  to have settlement systems that can work  fast that are reliable they already had  most of their sense of what money is in  the form of writing something down in  one document and transferring it to  another document so we can see  mechanisms ranging from like in turbine  settlement to cashing checks a lot of  these things have been electronic for a  long time but it was only in the 1970s  that people began to realize that this  kind of money electronic money digital  money ones and zeros as you say is going  to be everybody's money soon and that  that was a major social problem so now I  think that there's the idea that many  hold that cryptocurrency in its current  form is completely unique as a  phenomenon and in a sense I mean Bitcoin  is a relatively new thing specifically  but some of the the precedent for  Bitcoin like mechanisms do date back to  the 70s as you're talking about don't  they yes oh yeah absolutely  partially because people well maybe a  good way to put this is that when we  talk about a technology like Bitcoin  we're actually talking about something  that has a couple of different agendas  that it's trying to serve and when we  look at where digital cash stuff came  from we basically see those different  agendas at work so the earliest forms of  the underlying technologies that  eventually fed into Bitcoin where  digital cash projects that were just  designed to protect privacy they weren't  designed to like create a new form of  money they weren't designed to  necessarily let you do all of the you  know groovy new cryptocurrency stuff  people do now instead it was just a way  to say I want to exchange money with you  I want to buy something from you I want  to receive a gift from you that will  function like cash so that it will  create no record of the transaction  between us it will not need to know  anything about our identities it will  not enable any form of surveillance or  control to happen for that transaction  and so that's what we first see is the  idea of digital cash should be a  mechanism for protecting privacy when I  look at the sort of genesis of the  earlier forms of digital cash and also  more recently the cryptocurrency  movement it seems that there may be this  is a little bit too simplistic but there  are sort of two political ideologies  that seem to be particularly interested  there's the sort of libertarian side  which is about removing the government  as an intermediary privacy  direct transactions rather than the  intermediate you know intermediated  transactions and sort of like when you  look at Peter Thiel who was involved  with PayPal who talked about well maybe  PayPal could even actually develop a  currency that is free from government in  one sense that's the libertarian side I  also see and I'm curious whether you  agree there's the sort of like techno  utopian side which is just about aside  from government and privacy and all of  these other elements there's the  technology is just going to make  everything better side question one is  do you agree that those are sort of the  two political wings that seem most  interested number two are there others  and is there overlap between those two  those are excellent questions yeah I  think I think they I think the answer to  both is yes with question one obviously  one of the most kind of complicated  legacies of the way the digital cash  came to be that still shapes a lot of  aspects of how Bitcoin works a lot of  those technology decisions and a lot of  the kind of culture that still takes  place in the cryptocurrency space was  this interweaving of the libertarian  side and as you say like the techno  utopian side and the libertarian side  has its own very specific way of looking  at digital cash which is a view of the  world which is deeply suspicious of the  notion that the government should have  the right to mint money and therefore