encies  specifically do these seem like these  are going to be the long-term  replacements for cash or do they seem  more like intermediate experiments and  that the solution is ultimately going to  be elsewhere that's a really good  question um if I were to bet and I think  this is a pretty reasonable bet I think  we're entering an era of much more  diverse forms and types of money I think  we're going to see not a sort of one  ring to rule them all you know single  fixed solution that replaces everything  instead we're going to see a  proliferation of different kinds of  vehicles that can range from well to put  it in actually very straightforward  terms we can all relate to we're already  starting to live in that world because  we're starting to live in a world that's  shaped so much by points right like so  many of us are now used to the idea that  we have cards or accounts that carry  different kinds of value on them some of  which can be redeemed for cash some of  which can be redeemed for miles some of  which can be redeemed for minutes  depending on what country you're in and  what circumstances you're under so what  we are already kind of surrounded by  there's a much more diverse money system  that we don't necessarily think of as  being diverse than just cash and credit  and that's just going to become much  more so we're going to see a lot more  specialized types of money and types of  currency for different populations and  different groups so it's going to be  really interesting to see how that plays  out because I suspect that we will start  to understand ourselves as living in an  ecosystem of money all right  then Brunton doing an extremely good job  of keeping his cat out of the shot I can  tell the state behind the door Ben is an  associate professor of media culture and  communication at New York University  I've been really a pleasure speaking  with you today thank you so much David

